From 99f326470cde1216100691bec157c3a958b63988 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rautshivamxyz-beep <rautshivamxyz@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 03:50:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v9.33.152 "A store that could not be read is still not an
 empty store"

v9.33.119 gave NcieFiles.readOrNull two answers: "" means the file is not
there yet (a fresh install, nothing to lose) and null means it IS there and
could not be read (something to lose). It then made reminders, exams and
planner.json refuse to save over the second, because those three write back
what they read and adding one item to an empty list deletes everything else.

Four stores were still read-modify-written through a read that flattens both
answers to empty, and each of them loses real user state the same way:

  - experience.txt, the ledger of questions the user regenerated an answer to.
    One unreadable read persisted the new line as the entire ledger.
  - answer_cache.txt, up to 200 remembered answers. This is the one the user
    feels: the cache is what makes a repeat question instant, so losing it
    reads as NOVA getting slower and repeating itself for no reason.
  - context_value.txt, the per-source injected/helped counts that decide
    which sources earn their place in the prompt. Worse than a lost file: the
    flattened empty map was CACHED in `stats`, so one transient read error
    became the truth for the rest of the process, the tracker restarted from
    zero, and it began injecting sources it had already proved useless.
  - the grounding index, one `name<TAB>chunkCount` line per source. Writing
    the one changed source back over a failed read deleted every other line,
    and an article missing from the index is an article NOVA can no longer
    ground an answer in - on disk and invisible.

The writers ask now and refuse: NcieEngines gets a `writable` read that
returns null for "there and unreadable" while its readers keep the flattening
one, NcieValue stops caching a failed read and will not save a map built on
one, and indexUpdate leaves the index alone when it cannot read it. Every one
of them records the refusal through NovaLog.note, so the health screen's
tally hears about it - the v9.33.147 rule. A lost increment is recoverable; a
wiped store is not.

The checks hold it: no writer reads through the flattening read, every writer
refuses, the readers still flatten, and the four refusals each say so.
